# Chats — layout, thread UI memory and the command center

**Thread UI memory** (`chats/thread-ui-memory.ts`):

- Folds are remembered per thread, keyed by the row they belong to (`tools:<group id>`, `tool:<call id>`, …), never by an index.
- Only a press writes.
- Which hook remembers a boolean, in a thread or outside one: `.claude/rules/renderer-components.md` → _The one home for…_.
- Two things are deliberately not per thread: the context readout's drill-downs and the builder palette.
- The agents panel is always rendered when a chat is open and starts folded per thread. Nothing but its per-thread fold shows or hides it: no show/hide toggle, no header button, no auto-open on an artifact.
- The browser pane (`chats/browser/`) is a column of its own between the transcript and the agents panel, opened per thread by the agents panel's globe; it never takes the agents panel's place. It draws a placeholder that main covers with the chat's active tab (`browserSetBounds`), so every overlay primitive (`Dialog`, `Menu` — and `ContextMenu` and `Select` through it — and `Popover`) reports while it is open (`components/browser-occlusion.ts`), and while any is open anywhere the pane swaps the view for a still (`browserCapture`) — a new overlay that skips this is drawn under the page. A press on the still hands the keyboard to the page (`browserFocus`), since that is where the user meant to click. The one exception is the pane's own full-screen view (`BrowserColumn`), a `Dialog` with `coversBrowser={false}` that hosts the view itself while the column's pane steps aside. Open in new window moves the whole browser into a window of its own whose renderer draws only `BrowserPane` with `windowed` (`chats/browser/browser-window-root.tsx`, chosen in `main.tsx` before `App` loads); the docked pane keeps the tab strip and says the browser is in its own window, with Show window and Dock back. It opens by itself only for a tab an agent just opened (`BrowserStateEvent.agentOpenedTabId`), once, and when the browser docks back from its own window (`dockedBack`, so a column closed during the pop-out comes back); later agent actions only mark the globe. Every chat link (`useChatLink`, `ChatLink`, `ChatPanelLinkRow` in `chats/browser/chat-link.tsx`) opens an `http(s)` address in that pane on a plain click only while the pane is open, else in the system browser; its right-click menu offers both browsers and Copy link. A link whose address is a local absolute path (`localPathOfHref`: one leading `/`, percent-decoded) shows that path in Finder on a plain click instead (`useShowInFinder`, the artifact folder button's channel and gates: a fresh click, one per `OPEN_PATH_GAP_MS`, desktop only), titled Show in Finder, with why it was not shown beside it for `PATH_LINK_NOTICE_MS`; it has no right-click menu. A tab shows its name (`BrowserTabState.name`, else the page title, which stays in its tooltip) beside a colour dot that opens its colour and Rename menu (`components/color-rename-menu.tsx`, shared with the terminal's tabs; double-click the name renames too, through `TabStrip`'s `leading`, `editor` and `onDoubleClick`); the user's rename or recolour goes through `browserLabelTab` and main keeps it from every later agent label, field by field. On a paired device there is no pane and the globe says "The browser runs on your Mac". Tabs and cookies are each thread's own; the pane holds no browser state of its own beyond what main reports (`onBrowserState`).
- The agents panel's column holds only what belongs to the thread: its agents, teams, pull requests and pages. The run waterfall, the processes readout and the notes open in popups from its control row (and rail), never as sections in the column; the waterfall's and processes' reads are gated on that popup being open (`onPopupChange`), never on the panel being unfolded.
- The Progress window (`chats/progress-dialog.tsx`) is the thread's one overview and the chat screen's too: the summary (goals ring, headline, outcome, stage) on top, then tabs Timeline / Goals / Stages (Stages only in a team run). Timeline (`chats/thread-timeline.tsx`) is the user's messages and what happened on the thread, newest first by day — Key steps by default, Everything (calls, team notes, pages, achieved goals, plain approvals) remembered per thread (`timeline:everything`) — each row through the shared `components/timeline-event-row.tsx` the command center's timeline also draws; a row's press closes the window and jumps to its transcript row, an achieved goal shows the Goals tab, and a pull request's link is a sibling control beside the row's activator. The shelf's Progress chip and the agents panel's timeline icon open it on Timeline, the panel's Goals row on Goals; the tab lives on `Chats` beside the open flag, since the shelf renders under two parents. There is no timeline popover. The chip stands whenever the thread has stages, goals or an outcome, or the user can add a goal, and reads "Set goals" — the one status counter with words — while the user can add one and none is set; moving a stage stays in the panel's Stages section.
- The notes popup (`chats/notes-dialog.tsx`: the user's own notes, then a team run's team notes) is the chat screen's, not the panel's: the control row's notes button, the shelf's Team notes chip and a `team_note` row in the transcript (`TeamNoteOpenContext`) all open it, the last two on the pressed note.
- Read the MCP listing only when its disclosure opens (`mcpOpen`).

**Looks redundant, is not.** Check here before cutting:

- The task list renders in both the transcript and the agents panel. The user confirmed they want both.
- The shelf's Tasks, Sub-agents and Terminals chips restate panel counts on purpose.
- The chat-list row is four lines. Do not delete one.
- The tail `ResizeObserver` watches the scroller itself as well as its children, because a shrinking viewport fires nothing else.
- The composer shelf reserves its height (`min-h-7`). Anything thread-produced worth reaching is another chip there, never a new band — with one exception (user decision): messages other threads sent that still wait for this thread's turn are drawn above the composer as `chats/waiting-messages-strip.tsx` (sender, why it waits, first lines, Deliver now and Remove), because a counter alone went unnoticed; the Waiting messages chip lists them all. The working-agents card floats over the transcript instead, holding what a chip cannot: a row per agent and its messages. Content chips (changes, pull requests, buttons, teams) run durable to volatile from the left; the status counters (Terminals, Monitors, Sub-agents, Waiting messages, Team notes, Tasks, Progress) are glyph + figure only in `ComposerShelf`'s right-anchored `status` cluster, volatile to durable so the right edge stays put.

**Do not reintroduce:**

- Landing-view centring on the window.
- The run's folder in the composer: it belongs in the header.
